# CineMind — Multimodal Video Intelligence Platform
## Complete Project Context Document (AI Agent Handoff)

---

## 1. 🎯 Project Goal

CineMind is a **full-stack AI-powered web platform** where users upload any video (lecture, meeting, podcast, movie clip) and the platform:

- Transcribes speech with speaker identification and word-level timestamps
- Generates chapter-wise summaries with clickable timestamps
- Lets users **search inside the video** using natural language
- Lets users **chat with the video** — ask questions, get answers with exact timestamp references
- Visualizes a **sentiment/emotion timeline** across the entire video
- Auto-detects key highlights and important moments
- (Extra) Accepts YouTube URLs directly without manual upload
- (Extra) Auto-generates MCQ quizzes from video content
- (Extra) Exports summary as PDF

**Primary audience:** Students, researchers, professionals reviewing recorded meetings, content creators.  
**Purpose:** Placement/portfolio project demonstrating full-stack + multimodal AI skills.

---

## 2. 🏗️ System Architecture Overview

```
User Browser (Next.js)
       │
       ├──[REST API]──▶ FastAPI Backend (Render)
       │                      │
       │               ┌──────┴──────┐
       │            Celery        WebSocket
       │           Workers        (progress)
       │               │
       │        ┌──────┴──────────────┐
       │        │   AI Processing      │
       │        │  Pipeline (async)    │
       │        │                      │
       │        │ 1. Whisper (STT)      │
       │        │ 2. pyannote (diarize) │
       │        │ 3. SBERT (embed)      │
       │        │ 4. RoBERTa (emotion)  │
       │        │ 5. LLaVA (vision)     │
       │        │ 6. Llama3/Groq (LLM)  │
       │        └──────────────────────┘
       │
       ├── Supabase (PostgreSQL) — users, video metadata, job status
       ├── MongoDB Atlas — transcripts, chapters
       ├── ChromaDB — vector embeddings (transcript chunks)
       ├── Cloudflare R2 — raw video file storage
       └── Upstash Redis — Celery broker + caching
```

---

## 3. 🧰 Tech Stack — Every Tool & Its Role

### Frontend
| Technology | Role |
|-----------|------|
| **Next.js 14** | React-based frontend framework. Handles routing, SSR for fast loads, and connects to FastAPI via REST |
| **Tailwind CSS** | Utility-first CSS framework for styling all UI components |
| **Video.js** | Embeds the video player. Supports custom controls and programmatic timestamp seeking (clicking a chat answer jumps to that moment) |
| **ShadcnUI** | Pre-built component library (buttons, modals, cards) for consistent design |

### Backend
| Technology | Role |
|-----------|------|
| **FastAPI (Python)** | Main backend server. Handles all API endpoints: upload, status check, transcript fetch, chat, search |
| **Celery** | Distributed task queue. Offloads heavy AI work (transcription, embedding) to background workers so the API stays responsive |
| **Upstash Redis** | Acts as the message broker between FastAPI and Celery workers. Also used for caching frequent results |
| **WebSocket** | Pushes real-time job progress updates to the browser ("Transcription 45% complete...") |
| **JWT Auth** | JSON Web Tokens for user authentication. Google OAuth2 for social login |

### Databases
| Technology | Role |
|-----------|------|
| **Supabase (PostgreSQL)** | Stores structured data: user accounts, video records, job status, chapter metadata |
| **MongoDB Atlas** | Stores unstructured/variable-length data: full transcripts, speaker turns, sentence-level emotion tags |
| **ChromaDB** | Vector database. Stores 768-dimensional sentence embeddings of transcript chunks for semantic similarity search |

### AI / ML Models
| Technology | Role |
|-----------|------|
| **OpenAI Whisper (large-v3)** | Converts video audio to text. Produces word-level timestamps and handles multiple languages including Hindi |
| **pyannote.audio** | Speaker diarization — identifies and labels different speakers in the transcript ("Speaker 1", "Speaker 2") |
| **Sentence-BERT (all-MiniLM-L6-v2)** | Converts transcript text chunks into vector embeddings for storage in ChromaDB and semantic search |
| **RoBERTa (emotion classifier)** | Classifies each sentence into one of 7 emotion categories (joy, anger, sadness, fear, surprise, disgust, neutral) for the sentiment timeline |
| **LLaVA** | Multimodal vision model. Analyzes extracted video frames to understand visual content (slides, diagrams, on-screen text) |
| **Llama 3 70B via Groq API** | The LLM powering the chat engine. Receives retrieved transcript chunks as context (RAG) and generates answers with timestamps |
| **TextTiling Algorithm** | Unsupervised NLP algorithm that segments the transcript into topically coherent chapters |

### Storage & File Handling
| Technology | Role |
|-----------|------|
| **Cloudflare R2** | Cloud object storage for uploaded video files. S3-compatible API. Free 10GB storage + zero egress fees |
| **yt-dlp** | Python library to download YouTube videos from a URL — enables the YouTube import feature |

---

## 4. 🤖 AI Processing Pipeline (Step by Step)

When a video is uploaded, a Celery worker runs this pipeline:

```
Step 1: Extract audio from video using FFmpeg
Step 2: Run Whisper → get word-level transcript with timestamps
Step 3: Run pyannote.audio → assign speaker labels to each sentence
Step 4: Run RoBERTa → classify emotion for each sentence
Step 5: Extract 1 frame/second using OpenCV
Step 6: Run LLaVA on keyframes → generate visual descriptions
Step 7: Merge audio transcript + visual descriptions into enriched chunks
Step 8: Run TextTiling → segment into chapters
Step 9: Run Llama 3 on each chapter → generate chapter title + summary
Step 10: Run Sentence-BERT on all chunks → generate embeddings
Step 11: Store embeddings in ChromaDB
Step 12: Save all results to MongoDB + PostgreSQL
Step 13: Notify frontend via WebSocket → "Ready"
```

---

## 5. 💬 Chat with Video — RAG Pipeline

```
User types: "What was said about transformers at the end?"
       │
       ▼
Sentence-BERT encodes the query → query vector
       │
       ▼
ChromaDB cosine similarity search → top 5 matching transcript chunks
       │
       ▼
Chunks sent as context to Llama 3 via Groq API:
  System: "You are an assistant answering questions about a video.
           Use only the provided transcript context."
  Context: [chunk1 at 34:12, chunk2 at 41:05, ...]
  Question: "What was said about transformers at the end?"
       │
       ▼
Llama 3 generates answer with timestamp references
       │
       ▼
Frontend displays answer + clickable timestamps → clicking jumps video to that moment
```

---

## 6. 🗄️ Database Schema

### PostgreSQL (Supabase)
```sql
users(id, email, name, avatar_url, created_at)
videos(id, user_id, title, r2_url, duration_sec, status, created_at)
jobs(id, video_id, step, progress_pct, error, updated_at)
chapters(id, video_id, title, summary, start_time, end_time, order_index)
```

### MongoDB Atlas
```json
transcripts: {
  video_id, speaker, text, start_time, end_time, emotion, emotion_score, visual_context
}
```

### ChromaDB
```
Collection: video_chunks
Documents: { chunk_id, text }
Embeddings: [768-dim float vector]
Metadata: { video_id, start_time, end_time, speaker }
```

